      2. That sentence was baked into the journal step at the moment the scholar spoke,
         off wherever you happened to be standing, so it was wrong as soon as you moved.
      3. Arrival was a 14-tile test around a field of radius 12 — a two-tile skin on an
         invisible circle. Walking THROUGH the field and missing by three tiles reads
         exactly like "she does not exist".
    Now: a thread can carry a PLACE (`mark`) instead of a frozen sentence, and the
    journal recomputes the bearing every time it draws. `wayTo(x,y)` gives two legs — a
    live one off you and a fixed one off the nearest town, named however far away it is
    — so the step reads "south of you, the far side of the waste — southwest of
    DUSTPORT". Arrival is 22 at the field and 20 at the cairn. The mark rides the save.
    origin.js drove this whole quest before and passed, because it TELEPORTS the player
    onto the exact tile — it tested the mechanism and never the directions. It now walks
    to the field's edge instead, and checks the bearing changes from opposite corners of
    the map and names a real town. Three of those assertions fail on the old build.
    STILL WORTH DOING: `wayTo` is general and the other quests still use the old frozen
    `placeHint` line. Worth sweeping them onto `mark` too.

