[DONE] Binding Circle undead are just way too strong. They trivialize everything when summoned by a high level necromancer. We need to figure out how to balance them. Again, give me some IDEAS here and not just an immediate fix. Let’s brainstorm this.
    BUILT — see the three sections at the end of this item. The brainstorm is kept above
    them because its measurements are the record of what was wrong.

    THE BRAINSTORM. Measured by binding one of each type through the
    real `craftUndead` at magic 4/20/40/70/100/150 and pricing the exchange against real
    enemies through the real hitChance/atkPower/mitigate. Do not re-derive this:

    THE CAUSE IS TWO LINES THAT WERE NEVER MEANT TO MEET. `craftUndead` sets `m = magic*1.5`
    and scales BOTH `clawDmg` and `atk` on it. Four thousand lines away, the last multiplier
    in `atkPower` is `d *= 1 + c.stats.atk * 0.03`. So `atk` is not only a to-hit stat, it is
    a 3%-per-point DAMAGE multiplier — and the circle scales it alongside the claw. Two linear
    terms multiplied: damage is QUADRATIC in the necromancer's magic, against a world whose
    numbers are fixed constants.
    Measured damage per blow, unshaped, magic 4 -> 150:
      Gravehound      10 ->  545        Marrow Archer   16 -> 82
      Bone Knight     27 ->  984        Skeleton Mage    8 -> 17
      Ashen Colossus  53 -> 2101
    For scale, the hardest human in the game (Grand Marshal Vey) is 110 and never moves.
    A magic-100 Colossus kills a Bastion Paladin in 0.1 swings and needs 114 to die: a 926x
    edge, 1914x at lich cap. The note in the source blaming the shaping ceiling for the four
    colossi that solo'd the second Fracture is WRONG — an unshaped Colossus already does this.

    SECOND MECHANISM, and the one that makes it feel invulnerable rather than merely strong:
    bound `def` also scales on `m`, and `hitChance` clamps to [0.15, 0.94]. Past about magic 70
    every attacker in the world — boss or bandit — is floored at 0.15 against your dogs.

    THREE OF THE FOUR SCALING STATS ARE ALREADY DEAD WEIGHT. Toughness mitigation is
    `min(0.45, tough*0.006)`, maxed at tough 75; a Colossus reads 229. To-hit is dead past
    roughly enemy def + 20. Only the damage multiplier keeps paying, forever, which is exactly
    the wrong survivor. The panel is reporting numbers that do nothing.

    THE FIX TEMPLATE IS ALREADY IN THE FILE. `raiseDead` runs nearly the same arithmetic and
    then clamps it: `atk: Math.min(4 + m*0.7, bs.atk)`, with the comment "A corpse is never
    stronger dead than it was in life; the necromancer's skill only closes the gap." The circle
    never inherited that clamp because it builds from Mortal Remains rather than from a named
    body. Giving Remains a reference stat line to clamp against is the faithful fix and makes
    the whole carrion chain (Death Eater, Gravecart, Charnel, Ossuary) matter.
    DO NOT nerf flatly: at magic 4-20 these same units are genuinely WEAK (a Gravehound loses
    to a Paladin ten to one). What is missing is a plateau, not a smaller number.
    AND DO NOT TOUCH the seven utility bindings — Gravecart, Stitch-Hand, Death Eater,
    Soulbound, Wisp, Servitor, and the Mage's spell damage are all FLAT across the whole magic
    ladder and correctly priced. They are the part of the roster that works.

    ---- WHAT WAS THEN BUILT, 1 of 3: the harness, and remains as upkeep ----
    tools/host.js stands a raised host against a REAL town garrison — spawned through
    `spawnGuard`, so the arbalists and the merc mix are the game's — at four caster
    tiers, and reports the exchange rate in garrison dead per body of host lost. It
    also fights each host against its OWN NUMBER of guards, because the city figure
    conflates per-body power with the headcount the cap allows.
    THE BASELINE, measured before changing anything:
      m20   3 v 23 city -> wiped, killed nobody   ·  3v3 even -> 0.3:1
      m50   5 v 23 city -> 2.2:1                  ·  5v5 even -> FLAWLESS
      m100  7 v 23 city -> FLAWLESS               ·  7v7 even -> FLAWLESS
      lich 12 v 23 city -> FLAWLESS               · 12v12 even -> FLAWLESS
    Seven bound bodies erase a 23-strong garrison without a single loss, and the even
    fight shows per-body power is already runaway at m50, not just late. A Bone Knight
    goes atk 24 -> 141 across that range.
    UPKEEP is in, and split the way you asked: only `crafted` bodies (the binding
    circle) pay, at 0.25 Mortal Remains per point of bind weight per day. A body raised
    off a battlefield corpse pays NOTHING — `castRaise` never sets `crafted`, so the
    split was already there to key off. One gravehound costs 0.25/day; four Bone
    Knights cost 2.0/day; a late host of fifteen runs about 5.5/day, which is two or
    three bodies rendered. Run out and the cheapest body comes apart where it stands,
    one a day, never a lieutenant — a warning you can act on rather than a host that
    evaporates.
    HONEST NOTE: upkeep does NOT move the exchange rate, and was never going to — it is
    an economy lever, so it changes how long a host can be kept and how much corpse
    throughput it demands, not what happens when it meets a garrison. The harness still
    reports FLAWLESS at m100. Those numbers are printed as WATCH_ lines rather than
    assertions on purpose: failing the suite on a number the current change is not
    trying to move would make the suite lie. They become assertions when the `m` curve
    is bent, which is the change that actually moves them.

    ---- 2 of 3: levers 1 + 2 from "The Binding Circle Ledger" ----
    The artifact's audit was verified line by line against the source before anything was
    built, and every load-bearing claim held: `d *= 1 + atk * 0.03` at line 2085, hitChance
    clamped to [0.15, 0.94], toughness saturating at exactly 75 (0.45/0.006), and castRaise
    already carrying the clamp AND the comment that states the design intent.
    ITS DIAGNOSIS WAS SHARPER THAN THE ONE IN THE EARLIER BRAINSTORM, which called the curve
    "linear and uncapped". It is QUADRATIC: craftUndead scales both clawDmg and atk with the
    caster's magic, and atk is then a 3%-per-point damage multiplier four thousand lines away,
    so damage is a product of two linear terms. That interaction is the whole bug and the
    earlier pass missed it by reading the stat line and stopping there.

    LEVER 2 — the double-dip. `atk` was doing two jobs under one name: to-hit (which clamps at
    0.94 and stops paying around enemy def + 20) and a damage multiplier that never stops.
    Bound bodies now carry a SEPARATE `dmgAtk` for the multiplier, saturating, while `atk`
    itself keeps rising so they can still land a blow on plate. Nothing else in the world sets
    `dmgAtk`, so nothing else changed.

    LEVER 1 — the reference body. Mortal Remains carry a quality now: a running measure of the
    fighting worth of what was rendered into them, weighted by yield and multiplied by rot the
    same way castRaise does it. The binding clamps every stat to a ceiling derived from it.
    Kept as ONE rolling number rather than making remains non-fungible — a stash of
    individually-tracked bone is a much larger inventory change than this problem is worth.
    It deliberately does NOT live in `stash`: five separate places walk Object.keys(stash), and
    a quality figure in there would have rendered as a phantom item you could try to sell.
    Capped at 46 (about an elite mercenary) so the plateau cannot be farmed off by rendering
    bosses — without that the rule is unbounded, just slower.

    MEASURED, on a realistic bandit diet (before -> after):
      m20   0.3:1 even  ->  0.3:1     knight atk 24
      m50   FLAWLESS    ->  0.8:1     knight atk 35
      m100  FLAWLESS    ->  2.3:1     knight atk 35   (was 96)
      lich  FLAWLESS    ->  4.0:1     knight atk 35   (was 141)
    And the diet is now the ceiling, which is the point:
      fed rats     quality  9  ->  0.3:1   knight atk 20
      fed bandits  quality 22  ->  2.3:1   knight atk 35
      fed guards   quality 40  ->  FLAWLESS at 7v7   knight atk 56
      fed bosses   quality 46 (capped)     knight atk 63
    The early game is untouched, which was the constraint both the artifact and tools/host.js
    independently flagged: at magic 4-20 these bindings were already weak, so a flat nerf
    would have broken the early game to fix the late one.

    AND IT IS VISIBLE. The binding circle panel now states what the pile is worth, what that
    permits, and that rendering better dead raises it — a ceiling the player cannot see is not
    a mechanic, it is an unexplained nerf.

    ---- 3 of 3: the Marrow Archer grows its own bow ----
    The Sinew-Drawn half of Part II only — the archer grows its own bow now
    rather than consuming a crafted one. The other four proposals (Rib-Lattice, Quiver-Born,
    Splinter-Host, Cairn-Warden) were deliberately NOT built; this converts the existing unit
    instead of adding a line of them.
      Cost went from {remains:1, w_bow:1, wood:2} to {remains:2, wood:3, hide:1}, mana 14 to
    12. The bow it consumed had to be crafted at a Workbench from wood and fabric and hauled
    to the stores — six craft cycles before six archers, and the one recipe on the bench that
    reached outside the dark economy for a component.
      `w_sinew` is a real item with dmg 9 against the Hunting Bow's 11, a slower draw and
    effectively no armour-piercing: worse than the bought bow, free of the supply chain, and
    marked `grown` so it cannot be looted off the corpse, sold or stripped. It has its own
    mesh — the same bow in bone and gut.
      THE POINT IS THE CONSTANT. Measured across the magic ladder the archer's damage runs
    16.0 to 17.4 where a Bone Knight's triples, because the number comes off the weapon and
    not off `m`. That is the whole reason this unit was already balanced, and it is asserted
    now so a later change cannot quietly tie it to magic.
      ONE TRAP FOUND ON THE WAY: the archer draw animation tested
    `(wI.tierOf || c.weapon) === 'w_bow'` — a hardcoded item key — so ANY second bow in the
    game would have silently fallen through to `gun` and used the braced crossbow crouch. It
    asks `wI.bow` now.
    Also untouched: levers 3, 4, 5 (saturating m, cost scaling with output, mana/blood tax) —
    probably unnecessary now, but host.js will say so.

[OPEN] For the shaping “sliders” in the binding circle (size, mind, etc.) — those should have a set ceiling independent of caster skill. A lich is capable of creating godly undead with almost maxed shaping across the board. Keep the ceiling at the base level so these represent a genuine tradeoff.
    NOT STARTED, and the arithmetic says you are right by a wider margin than it looks.
    `shapeCeiling(caster)` is `shapeBudget(caster) + 4`, and `shapeBudget` is
    `12 + floor(magic/12) + 2 (necromancy) + 2 (ossuary_rites)`. Measured:
      magic   4 -> budget 16, ceiling 20
      magic 100 -> budget 24, ceiling 28
      magic 150 (lich) -> budget 28, ceiling 32
    SHAPE_AXES is six axes and SHAPE_MAX is 5, so maxing EVERY axis costs 30. A lich's ceiling
    is 32. It can therefore max all six with two points to spare, which is precisely the "no
    tradeoff at all" you are describing — and a living necromancer at the skill cap is at 28
    of 30, which is barely better. The tradeoff only exists in the early game.
    The change is small: hold `shapeCeiling` at a constant (SHAPE_BUDGET_BASE + 4 = 16 is the
    obvious candidate, and 16 against a 30-point board is a real choice) and leave
    `shapeBudget` scaling as it is, so skill keeps buying you the same shape more CHEAPLY
    without buying you a better one. That preserves what the budget is for and kills what the
    ceiling has become. Both are enforced in two places — the panel disables the `+` button
    and `craftUndead` re-checks — so both want the same constant.
    WHILE YOU ARE IN THERE: the shape initialiser and the EVEN button both write
    `{mass, force, haste, knit, plate, quiet, will}`. `quiet` and `will` were merged into MIND
    and are no longer in SHAPE_AXES, so those two keys are written, saved, and never read —
    and `mind` is absent from both literals, arriving only via the defaulting loop underneath.
    Harmless today, confusing later.

[DONE] No materials should spawn INSIDE the towns — distracting, and makes it tougher to maneuver.
    Decor was cleared to a flat 15 tiles around a town centre while the walls run to 27, so
    every walled town had trees, boulders and ore outcrops growing between r15 and its own
    rampart — inside the gates, in the streets. Measured at 392 decor tiles standing inside
    town walls across the map. Cleared to the wall plus three now (`clearR` on the town), and
    the sweep radius is stored rather than derived because rawDecorAt is called for every tile
    the renderer and the pathing touch.
    ONE THING THIS BROKE AND HOW IT WAS FIXED: the rich seam that gives Copperhold and Ironscar
    their identity is centred 13 tiles from the town with a radius of 15-17. That cleared the
    old sweep by a whisker and sits wholly inside the new one, so widening it deleted both
    mining towns' ore outright — `findNode` came back NONE for iron from every town in the
    world. The seam is pushed out past the wall now, where a pit belongs anyway. That left MORE
    ore than before (80 iron-vein tiles to 114), because the seam lands on ground nobody sweeps.

[DONE] Rocky patches of ground shouldn't be minable for stone — click-moving to a mountain
       defaults to harvesting it. Keep stone on the world's rocks only, so it stays finite and
       can be mined out locally.
    Rocky TERRAIN (tile type 2) counted as a stone source in three places: the worker's
    `findNode` search, the click resolver `gatherKindAt`, and an explicit exemption in the
    gather tick that stopped terrain-stone from ever depleting. All three now require an actual
    `rock` boulder. That is what made every mountainside a quarry — and a mountain is the
    biggest thing on the map, so a click meant to walk near one landed on "mine that instead".
    It also makes stone genuinely finite, which is what you asked for: a boulder is a node with
    a cap of 14 uses and a slow regrow, so a place can be worked out. There are ~43,000 boulders
    in the world and the nearest one to a town is 17-31 tiles, so stone is plentiful without
    being underfoot.

NEW ENEMIES FOR THE LATER GAME.  Specifications, not tasks — nothing here has
been started.  Both are Watcher-kin and both want the GAUNTS table, which is the
one place a new hostile body is cheap to add: a row of stats plus a `claw`, a
