[OPEN] Eyes of Ainzopha’ar: swarms of floating eyes. Their gaze lights heretics aflame with holy fire. (Ranged enemy, incorporates destruction alchemy.)
    NOT STARTED. The name is already load-bearing lore — AINZOPHA'AR'S LIGHT is the divine
    warding spell, the small terrible sun that gaunts will not approach. Something named for
    that carrying holy fire against YOU is a good inversion and should probably be written to
    read as one.
    Mechanically the closest thing that exists is the Wisp: `beast` + a small `big` + no
    humanoid rig, which is how a floating body is already done. For the gaze, note the
    Skeleton Mage is the existing pattern for a body that casts and only casts — `gift`,
    `onlyArt`, an `arts` map and `stance:'ranged'` — so an Eye is roughly a hostile Wisp body
    with the Mage's casting wiring. `emberrot` (EMBERS OF AVARICE, a fire that keeps burning)
    is the obvious art and already exists.
    SWARM is the part to think hardest about, because it is the part the game does not have.
    Everything hostile currently spawns as individuals with a squad id. A swarm that behaves as
    one thing is either a lot of cheap bodies (watch the `charsNear` cost and the squad-bar
    collision the log-lift item was about) or one body that renders as many. Decide which
    before writing anything — they are completely different builds.
    Balance note: read the binding-circle findings above first. A ranged enemy that ignores
    armour is exactly the counter to a host whose survivability comes from `def` flooring
    everything at 0.15, so this creature is a real lever on the necromancy balance problem and
    should be designed with that in mind rather than by accident.

[OPEN] Messengers: intelligent Watchers, speaking an ancient tongue no one understands or remembers. Variable stats, capable of learning and growing, and they come in multiple varieties — alchemist and warrior classes. They do not use technology, relying on latent eldritch power to wield weapons composed of fire and light. (Their weapons are not lootable, but unique to them.) Late game, some walk among the Paladins.
    NOT STARTED. This one is more interesting than it looks because "capable of learning and
    growing" is a thing the game already simulates and currently gives to nobody hostile:
    `learnMult` on a body multiplies every `xpGain`, and the MIND shaping axis is the only
    thing that sets it. Put `learnMult` on a Messenger and it genuinely gets better over a
    long campaign, which no other enemy in the world does. That, not the stat line, is the
    creature — and it means they should be spawned EARLY and rarely so there is time for it
    to happen, rather than being introduced at full strength late.
    Existing pieces to build on:
      - `gauntKind` for what they are; they should count as Watchers for the cull/news tables
        (CULL_FOES already maps gaunt -> 'watchers').
      - `onlyArt` for the alchemist class — attCap already returns 3 for the one art and 0 for
        the rest, so a Messenger that knows fire and can never know anything else is free.
      - Unlootable weapons: `dropItems` is the drop table and a body with no `weapon` but a
        `clawDmg` already fights with what it is rather than what it carries. A weapon of fire
        and light is most cheaply an unarmed body with a high claw and a `wt` of 'burn', which
        also means it can never be disarmed or looted by construction rather than by a flag.
      - "Some walk among the Paladins": the Purge is the `purge` faction and already spawns
        Paladins at the Bastion and in roaming bands. A Messenger placed in those spawns is a
        faction line, not a new system — but note the Purge burns people for the profane gift,
        so a Watcher standing in their ranks is a story beat and should probably be something
        the player can notice and react to rather than a silent stat upgrade.
    The ancient tongue: `barks` is already per-body and already used for the Redoubt units.
    Untranslated speech costs nothing and is the cheapest characterful thing on this list.
